Because marijuana is still illegal under federal law, sending any amount of marijuana through the USPS is a no-no. This is true even if you live in a legalized state and are shipping to a legalized state.
Sending a controlled substance via mail can result in harsh consequences. Since the 1970s, marijuana has been classified as a Schedule 1 substance by the federal government.
Currently, the base offense for shipping less than 50 kilograms of marijuana carries a potential sentence of five years in jail and a fine of $250,000.
The FBI’s sanctions for cannabis sender and recipient have not been decreased as a result of the legalization of marijuana in some jurisdictions.
Many law enforcement authorities have stated that they do not want to waste time on little marijuana cases, so if you do transport pot, make sure it is carefully wrapped and packaged. What Is the Chance of Getting Caught While Mailing Weed?
More than 3000 people are detained each year by US Postal inspectors on suspicion of dealing in counterfeit money or drug trafficking. Around 62,000 Ibs of marijuana were collected in 2014, with 3,299 people jailed.
For starters, receiving marijuana through mail is unquestionably safer than purchasing it from a dodgy dealer on the street. When it comes to obtaining illegal substances, there’s always the possibility that something will go wrong as a result of the product’s restriction, whether it’s being assaulted by a shady dealer or receiving some awful cannabis that will give you a headache for the rest of the day.
Which one is right for your business?
In times when you need to make a cannabis entity selection, you can find three main business structures that differ in your rights and obligations. In the cannabis business, you can consider an LLC, C corporation (C-corp), or S corporation (S-corp).
On the other hand, there are two forms of corporations that have their advantages. With an S-corp, the business doesn’t pay the corporate income tax, and personal assets can be protected.
With the S-corp, the owners pay personal income tax on profits, and the business must not pay corporate income tax, which is not the case with C-corp where the business must pay corporate income tax. At the same time, all the income or loss is passed through to owners each year with the S-corp.
