Far away from any civilization on a seemingly untouched island, the Dahan (natives) still live with the spirits in harmony and harmony.
Unlike in the rest of the world, in this case we don't play the settlers but the ghosts who drive the settlers off their island.
Each ghost begins with a deck of various ability cards that it uses to control its attacks and actions. At the beginning of each ghost phase, each player chooses one or more actions (depending on his chosen ghost), whereby he can, for example, expand his influence and thus either play more cards in a round or receive more energy at the beginning of his round.
Faster are executed before the invaders phase and slower after it.
The common planning and support of the spirits among themselves is the way to the goal, where some spirits specialize in fear can better defend others. Cooperative action is always the key to victory.
after drawing there.
Each ghost actually plays itself totally differently and brings its own talents with it to go to war against the invaders.
Personally, I think the game is also very successful thematically, because you don't just play the settlers again and build your city, but have to prevent that.
If you want to complain about something then it is the playing time, if at all, but I don't think you can give a minus point for this. Due to the planning, Spirit Island is a very time-consuming deck building game, which does not disappoint even after playing several times, as you can also work hard on the difficulty level from easy to ultra.
